		{"id":"f7218b6810e442a2","type":"group","x":3133,"y":-6000,"width":2707,"height":2060,"label":"Random Questions"},
		{"id":"f74c4ec8eb9b81e2","type":"text","text":"Data Complexity","x":-4035,"y":-1940,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1574a751f59ccb66","type":"text","text":"Intrinsic Dimensions\n- [The Intrinsic Dimension of Images and Its Impact on Learning](http://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08894)\n\t- estimating the intrinsic dimensions(ID) of image datasets(iclr 2021). ID has direct correlation to the complexity of learning as stated in this paper.\n\t- Imgnet complexity shown to be much higher compared to cifar. \n\t- They even verified their method using GAN generated synthetic datasets.\n- ","x":-4300,"y":-1812,"width":700,"height":789},
		{"id":"668c66b0dff48010","type":"text","text":"Interesting Ideas","x":-3040,"y":-2180,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"548a79f8142ab661","type":"text","text":"Param-Space Based DEF","x":-2240,"y":-1840,"width":292,"height":55},
		{"id":"0f688446ec9d2839","type":"text","text":"### Foundational Works?\n1. [CLIP is an Eff Cont Learner](https://openreview.net/forum?id=qZ1yqKvQtE) - \n\t - claims to be the first to show the compatibility of clip for cl. \n\t - did a lot of ablation on the raw zero-shot power of clip for CIL, TIL and DIL experiments\n2. [Overcoming CF](http://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00796)  \n\t- seminal paper on CF and Continual Learning from 2017\n\t- Proposed Elastic Weight Consolidation based on how neuron activity in brains work\n\t- This seems to be the one which led to regularisation based methods.\n\t- emphasised on the need for the network to be **overparameterized** to find a set of params for task b that also fit for task A.","x":-3440,"y":-3360,"width":780,"height":860},
		{"id":"6abf4d146edb72e1","type":"text","text":"- Joint Effect of Task Similarity and Over-parameterization on CatForg\n\t- Recent work suggest that ContL is **easiest when task sim is high or low** and most **difficult for intermediate levels** in overparameterised models\n\t- CF increases monotonically in critical param models with task dissim.\n\t- They did MLP runs on MNIST/EMNIST and verified their analysis.\n\t- Can be extended to more complex non-linear models-CNNs, Trans\n\t- Other notions of sim, image classification CL,\n- How much Do LMs Memorise\n\t- Work by Google. Got bit wise memory capacity for LMs\n\t- Mem = Unintended Mem + generalisation\n\t- Used Shannon and Kolgomorov Information Theory concepts. I(x;y) and  H(x).\n- [Forgetting in Language Models: Capacity, Optimization, and Self-Generated Replay](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.26097)\n\t- show that model capacity affects forgetting.\n\t- Overtrained models forget more.\n\t- They tested with only a single new task, so multi-task scenarios and the loss of plasticity is not explored.\n- [ Knowledge Entropy Decay during Language Model Pretraining Hinders New Knowledge Acquisition](http://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01380)\n\t- Formulate expression for knowledge entropy\n\t- based on the structure of FFNs. Make use of the FFN layers in the models to calculate this value. Also referred to some similar entropy metrics.\n\t- knowledge entropy decreases with model pre-training and seems to be correlated directly to data acqui ability and cata-forgetting. less entropy => more forgetting & less plasticity.\n\t- Artificially increasing the knowledgy entropy by modifying weight values in the layers seems to aid in both plasticity and stability but not exactly the best way it seems. as model is still worse in those metrics compared to an earlier pre-training checkpoint.``","x":-3280,"y":-1700,"width":840,"height":1140},
		{"id":"e4bb48cafeda6da9","type":"text","text":"Understanding the limits of Plasticity","x":-2981,"y":-1825,"width":242,"height":81},
		{"id":"e7ed52b4b300a0d5","type":"file","file":"Research Work/VAL-DEL/Open Questions.md","x":-2320,"y":-3380,"width":975,"height":980},
		{"id":"53e33eeae07bba8f","type":"text","text":"- KeepLORA: CL with Residual Gradients\n\n\n\n- Parameter Isolation to protect task knowledge\n\t- CL without parameter isolation. Basically, instead of freezing neurons relevant for each task and training the rest for the other task.\n    - Essentially train fresh model on the new task and then fuse it with the older model - some graph-matching method they used. \n    - Underlying concept is to dedicate different neural pathways(sequence of weights and activation operations from input till output) for each task.\n    - They show this by plotting the final output activation corresponding to each task. Non-overlapping groups of output activation were shown to be high for each task . They got good results on task-aware and task-agnostic scenarios for CIFAR-100 and TInyImgnet","x":-2282,"y":-1700,"width":860,"height":880},
		{"id":"ae49e985585b790e","type":"text","text":"Important","x":-1957,"y":-3540,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"c3c4848261a21579","type":"file","file":"Misc/VAL Notebook.md","x":-1180,"y":-4160,"width":820,"height":620},
		{"id":"6622c8e22cf8184a","type":"text","text":"## Does Memorizing Facts Lead to Better Generalization?\nsource: [\\[2604.08519\\] Cram Less to Fit More: Training Data Pruning Improves Memorization of Facts](http://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08519)\n\nMemorizing facts—especially those in the \"long tail\" of a data distribution—is actually considered **theoretically necessary for accurate learning and generalization**. Because real-world data typically features a long-tailed distribution (meaning there are a few very common concepts and a massive number of rare ones), models must be able to memorize data containing rare knowledge to accurately learn and generalize to those rare situations.\n\nHowever, the relationship between fact memorization and overall generalization involves some key trade-offs, particularly when drawing parallels to \"hard-to-learn\" samples:\n\n- **The tension between rote memorization and complex reasoning:** The researchers highlight an interesting split in training strategies. To maximize pure fact accuracy, their method deliberately selects \"easy\" or low-loss data (facts that are highly memorizable and within the model's capacity). Conversely, they point out that other well-known data selection methods do the exact opposite—they prioritize high-loss, \"hard-to-learn\" samples. Prioritizing difficult samples is often better for **complex learning tasks that require deeper generalization and reasoning**, making it clear that an algorithm optimized just for memorizing independent facts may not be ideal for building broader reasoning skills.\n- **General capabilities can be preserved during pruning:** Even though these goals can conflict, the authors demonstrate that if you carefully prune a dataset to match a model's capacity—specifically by filtering out excessively rare (hard) facts or overly redundant ones—you can drastically boost a model's fact memorization **without harming its general natural language understanding (NLU)**. Their experiments show that general capabilities, like commonsense reasoning, remain stable even when the training data is heavily pruned to favor fact retention.\n- **Risks of overfitting and catastrophic forgetting:** Pushing a model to memorize facts can negatively affect its broader utility if it leads to overfitting or privacy leaks. Furthermore, forcing a model to memorize _new_ facts later in training (such as during finetuning) often causes \"catastrophic forgetting,\" where the model overwrites its parameters and inadvertently degrades its previously learned knowledge and generalizations.\n\nIn summary, memorizing facts is a foundational requirement for generalizing across diverse, long-tailed data. However, there is a distinct trade-off: training regimens that focus on difficult, hard-to-learn samples are better for teaching complex reasoning and generalization, while regimens that focus on easier, low-loss samples are optimal for pure fact memorization.","x":3160,"y":-5960,"width":647,"height":681},
		{"id":"6228fcee9b4b7c6a","type":"text","text":"###  Interpreting Theory for Scaling Laws for Data Pruning in Vision\n- [Scaling Laws from the Data Manifold Dimension](http://jmlr.org/papers/v23/20-1111.html) \n\t- Maybe searching deeper into their analysis of hypercubes being formed in the underlying data manifold.\n\t- Based on that analysis they derive the scaling law for the mse and cross-ent losses\n- [ The Quantization Model of Neural Scaling](http://arxiv.org/abs/2303.13506) \n\t- Hypothesises that there are *quantas/subtasks*  of knowledge/data that the models must learn.\n\t- Some quanta more useful at reducing the loss\n\t- The use frequency of a quanta is distributed based on the Zipf distribution. Power law scaling comes from the amount of quanta learnt\n\t\t- they derive model size power law, data size law, training steps law.\n\t\t- They have assumed samples to be monogenic.(Images would be polygenic/multi-feature)\n\t- \n- [Superposition Yields Robust Neural Scaling \\| OpenReview](https://openreview.net/forum?id=knPz7gtjPW)\n\t- Derives the scaling law w.r.t to the model size(which itself is a function of the latent dimension) based on the theory of sup.erposition among representations. Infinite data regime.\n\t- Says there are two regimes weak superpos and strong superposition\n\t- In weak superpositions only the highest frequency features hold value and loss scales with power law of model size if the feature frequency distribution is power dist.\n\t- In strong superposition, if the feature distributions are less skewed(more evenly spread) then power law holds as the inverse of m. But is very skewed then power law holds only if the feature dist is power dist.\n- [Scaling Laws in Patchification: An Image Is Worth 50,176 Tokens And More \\| OpenReview](https://openreview.net/forum?id=uEsWuHra1Y)\n\t- Reducing the patch size provides a better computation-accuracy trade-off and a superior scaling limit than simply scaling up the model's parameters\n\t- increasing the hidden dimension does almost nothing to improve reconstruction loss, but shrinking the patch size to 1x1 drops the loss dramatically \n\t- **Information loss is primarily due to patch size**","x":1680,"y":-2040,"width":1120,"height":940},
		{"id":"75caa055b353751c","type":"text","text":"## Spurious Correlations\n- [The Impact of Coreset Selection on Spurious Correlations and Group Robustness](http://arxiv.org/abs/2507.11690)\n\t- The previous paper builds up on this one from neurips 25.  \n\t- they study how different coreset selection policies affect the dataset bias and the robustness of training(gen/test loss)\n\t- main point is to max gen acc, not the avg accuracy.\n\t- Add a lot of nuances to the previous knowledge.\n    - How low bias does not necessarily mean more robustness.\n    - How difficult-based pruning only leads to more robustness till a particular value of the pruning ratio.\n\t- balancing of bias groups(not classes) can lead to better robustness and less overall bias. this is again conditional on the difficulty of the samples in the group.\nA lot more of this work can be extended. Analysis with the newer pruning metrics can be done since they only did el2n,uncertainty, forgetting , etc. Also maybe with dynamic pruning metrics which might be better for this balancing scenario due to their dynamic nature??  \nAlso once again, since their focus was on datasets with inherent biases , they used the waterbirds/similar data.  Maybe an analysis over cifar or imagenet can be fruit-full?  \n  \nAlso they again point out that for very low ratios, difficult does not yield good results. Easy or random selection does better. Similar to what was kinda pointed by maini.  \nBetter definitions of \"group robustness\" which are less coarse and more fair might a direction to work as suggested by them. (edited) \n- [\\[2606.02830v1\\] Mitigating Spurious Correlations with Memorization-Guided Dataset De-Biasing](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.02830v1)\n\t- Maybe the inclusion of spurious correlations in the coreset could be what is leading to the failure of the data pruning scaling law? Most coreset selection methods don't filter out samples with spurious correlations from the coreset, which leads to high test/generalisation error.  \n\t\t- Basically if you have a group with + bias and another group with -ve bias in the same proportion in your pruned dataset, the model should learn the core features instead of the spurious ones.\n\t- They observed that both EL2N scores and clip similarity, were greatly driven by the background and not the core attributes of the subjects(birds)\n\t- In this paper, they provided a way to de-bias the coreset and include only \"good\" samples which lead to generalisation. Not  tested on cifar or imagenet tho. But mostly on datasets which actually have these kinds of spurious relations like Waterbird which often have water, or forest in the background. They however got sota(and beyond) results on these with 10% data only.\n\t- They first trained a model $f_s$on the spurious features for a few epochs under the assumption that the spurious features are learnt first and hence lowest loss. They then upweight the ones with the lowest low such that they influence the training the most.\n\t- Then they train the core model $f_c$ with a modified loss function using the frozen $f_s$ models' outputs.\n\t- After these are trained, they used their loss values as the scores for the group robust pruning.\n\t- Samples with high TCSLs scores(*without strong spurious features*) are grouped into $G_high$, from which we randomly select samples until the quota min $|G_{high}|, r|D|$ is filled.","x":1680,"y":-960,"width":1160,"height":1040},
		{"id":"a2c18561936a623f","type":"text","text":"### Scaling Laws for Data Filtering\n- [Scaling Laws - data prune not comp agnostic](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10656415/)\n\t- Emphasised on sample utility decaying with sample repetition.\n\t- Provided a derivation for explaining the power law in the context of multiple-data pools each having a seperate utility.\n\t- *Has an effective dataset interpretation which might connect and extend to the lang stat paper*.\n- [ Beyond neural scaling laws: beating power law scaling via data pruning](http://arxiv.org/abs/2206.14486) -  [[Beyond Neural Scaling Law - Data Pruning improve power law]]\n\t- In the toy model, showed that with data-pruning but fixed compute you can acheive exponenential scaling of loss\n\t- They provided a pareto frontier on the scaling curves which gives you the ideal retention fraction of data(under the assumption of the proper pruning metric) for  a  an effective dataset interpretation which might connect and extend to the lang stat paper\n\t- They also showed how exponential to power scaling transformation happens as the prune metric becomes more non-ideal.\n- [ Deriving Neural Scaling Laws from the statistics of natural language](http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07488)\n\t- Derived Scaling laws purely based on the language statistics\n\t- Decomposed Loss into a one term which depends on the max backwards context used \n\t- Other term of the loss was due to the underutilisation of the *actual* context\n- [\\[2602.00315v2\\] Beyond the Loss Curve: Scaling Laws, Active Learning, and the Limits of Learning from Exact Posteriors](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00315v2)\n- [ Data pruning and neural scaling laws: fundamental limitations of score-based algorithms](http://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06960)\n\t- Work is based on the assumption of an infinite pools of initial dataset.\n\t- Their analysis and proposed improvements on the static data pruning methods has similarity to the *OrderDP* paper.\n\t- The paper investigates why popular *Score-Based Pruning Algorithms* (SBPAs) like GraNd, DeepFool, and Uncertainty fail in the \"high compression regime\" (when keeping 30% or less of the original data)\n\t- The pruning methods(static ones seen here) are rigid, throw away large parts of the continuous data distribution and lead to a distribution shift. Mathematically distorts the landscape.\n\t- In low pruning zones, the data still has enough diversity but in the high pruning zone there is too less of it leading to this bias.\n\t- They *proved* that *static methods won't work beyond a fixed threshold*, that is even scaling the intial data won't lead to convergence to the ideal loss.\n\t- They showed that calibration - having a small part of the budget to randomly selected samples from the rejected pile, - improves that rigid pruning. this portion is again controlled by a hyper-param.\n\t- They showed(verified and proved) that *SBPA pruning fail* to beat **Random sampling** for the same overall data/compute budget.\n- ","x":-227,"y":-1760,"width":1427,"height":1120},
		{"id":"bfdb9807beac198f","type":"text","text":"#data_efficient_ml \n#### Static \n- El2N \n\t- L2 norm of loss\n- Dyn-Unc\n\t- Moving std deviation of output prob\n- Moderate-Coreset\n\t- selecting points that are at a moderate distance from the class center. L2 Distance in the embedding space.\n- InfoMax\n\t- Requires a quadratic solver. \n\t- Finds a subset of samples that *maximizes overall information* by simultaneously considering each sample’s information contribution and also minimizing the  *information overlap/redundancy among them*.\n- [Unc + Diff Metric icml26](http://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06905)  \n\t- combines dyna-unc and difficulty metric to a new metric. Has a Beta-sampling version which slowly shifts the policy to sampling easier samples as the pruning ratio goes up. It helps maintain diversity in the pruned set.\n\t- Best among stat pruning methods especially with the beta-sampling mode. imagent results almost beat sota but they did using resnet34.\n\t- filters out the label noised samples well too. It is also net compute(training + score compute) optimal compared to the other static metrics\n- [D2 Pruning: Message Passing for Balancing Diversity and Difficulty in Data Pruning](http://arxiv.org/abs/2310.07931)\n- \n\n#### Dynamic\n- Usually the ranking score is loss-based due to dynamic scenario\n- Info-Batch:\n\t- maintain a moving average of the scores of data points across training\n\t- prune out points with scores under this avg value with prob r.\n\t- scale up the gradients of these remaining valid but unpruned points to approx match the original grad to remove the bias generated due to pruning\n- OrderDP\n\t- Discarded Data can cause bias in the training gradients. the scaling in info-batch doesn't remove all the bias, in fact can trigger more bias in certain aspects due to the scale up factor. \n\t- Algo is simple, randomly prune oubetter than info-batcht the low loss samples at each training iteration and train only on the top-q ones.\n\t- mathematical guarantee of convergence.\n\t- better than info-batch\n-","x":-1080,"y":-3141,"width":1140,"height":901},
		{"id":"7c98704f6c07930b","type":"file","file":"Research Work/VAL-DEL/Quantization Based Hypothesis.md","x":3080,"y":-2480,"width":840,"height":600},
